1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,530
On October sixth, 2026, one company was worth five point eight trillion dollars.

2
00:00:07,559 --> 00:00:10,210
More than the entire economy of Japan.

3
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:14,769
Five weeks later, one point seven trillion of it was gone.

4
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:17,010
Not because the chips stopped selling.

5
00:00:17,239 --> 00:00:21,289
Last quarter, Nvidia made ninety-six billion dollars.

6
00:00:21,239 --> 00:00:23,410
More than double the year before.

7
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:25,089
The stock fell anyway.

8
00:00:25,559 --> 00:00:27,329
First, interest rates.

9
00:00:27,559 --> 00:00:31,129
Then, doubts about whether AI spending pays off.

10
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,530
And then, an email from the man who built it all.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:43,329
For thirty years, Jensen Huang has told his employees the same thing: "Our company is thirty days from going out of business."

12
00:00:43,159 --> 00:00:45,490
It always sounded like a motto.

13
00:00:45,359 --> 00:00:49,810
This month, Wall Street is asking whether it is a forecast.

14
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:54,049
This is the case against the most valuable company in history.

15
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:55,490
Five cracks.

16
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:58,450
And what it would actually take for Nvidia to fall.

17
00:01:00,347 --> 00:01:05,278
If you have a 401(k) or an index fund, you own Nvidia.

18
00:01:05,308 --> 00:01:08,837
It is one of the biggest pieces of the S&P 500.

19
00:01:08,828 --> 00:01:12,078
When it moves, your retirement moves with it.

20
00:01:12,108 --> 00:01:15,718
And it got that big astonishingly fast.

21
00:01:15,787 --> 00:01:19,677
For twenty years Nvidia made graphics cards for gamers.

22
00:01:19,707 --> 00:01:27,837
Then ChatGPT arrived, and it turned out that artificial intelligence runs best on exactly the chips Nvidia makes.

23
00:01:27,867 --> 00:01:35,197
In three years, its yearly sales went from twenty-seven billion dollars to two hundred and sixteen billion.

24
00:01:35,227 --> 00:01:39,397
It keeps about seventy-five cents of every dollar as gross profit.

25
00:01:39,468 --> 00:01:44,518
It became the one company selling shovels in the biggest gold rush in history.

26
00:01:44,587 --> 00:01:49,558
And when you are the only shovel seller, everyone has a reason to want you gone.

27
00:01:51,474 --> 00:01:53,564
Start with who buys the shovels.

28
00:01:53,875 --> 00:01:58,084
A huge share of Nvidia's sales comes from a handful of giants.

29
00:01:58,114 --> 00:01:59,165
Microsoft.

30
00:01:59,155 --> 00:02:00,125
Amazon.

31
00:02:00,194 --> 00:02:00,924
Google.

32
00:02:01,075 --> 00:02:02,045
Meta.

33
00:02:02,114 --> 00:02:04,204
Now put yourself in their seat.

34
00:02:04,275 --> 00:02:10,245
You send Nvidia tens of billions of dollars a year, and you know most of it is profit margin.

35
00:02:10,275 --> 00:02:12,085
So you do the obvious thing.

36
00:02:12,115 --> 00:02:13,965
You build your own chip.

37
00:02:14,034 --> 00:02:15,965
Every one of them is.

38
00:02:15,794 --> 00:02:20,965
And Google's are now good enough that other AI companies are choosing them over Nvidia.

39
00:02:21,155 --> 00:02:23,884
Nvidia's defense is its software.

40
00:02:23,875 --> 00:02:28,284
Millions of developers are trained on it, and switching is painful.

41
00:02:28,074 --> 00:02:31,965
But Nvidia does not have to lose these customers to get hurt.

42
00:02:31,794 --> 00:02:34,205
They only need a choice.

43
00:02:34,034 --> 00:02:40,205
Because the moment a customer has a choice, that seventy-five percent margin becomes a negotiation.

44
00:02:42,105 --> 00:02:45,156
The second crack is about where the money comes from.

45
00:02:45,545 --> 00:02:47,316
Follow one dollar.

46
00:02:47,385 --> 00:02:49,955
Nvidia invests in an AI company.

47
00:02:50,265 --> 00:02:53,316
That company uses the money to rent data centers.

48
00:02:53,545 --> 00:02:56,596
Those data centers are filled with Nvidia chips.

49
00:02:56,825 --> 00:03:00,515
The dollar comes home, and this time it counts as sales.

50
00:03:00,825 --> 00:03:03,235
Critics call it circular financing.

51
00:03:03,466 --> 00:03:05,075
And it is not small.

52
00:03:05,306 --> 00:03:10,955
Nvidia has put thirty billion dollars into OpenAI, the maker of ChatGPT.

53
00:03:10,985 --> 00:03:19,036
Its own filings show it has guaranteed up to a hundred and five billion dollars in lease payments on data centers built for OpenAI.

54
00:03:19,066 --> 00:03:23,156
If OpenAI cannot pay, Nvidia is on the hook.

55
00:03:23,306 --> 00:03:25,036
We have seen this before.

56
00:03:25,066 --> 00:03:30,915
In the late nineties, the companies building the internet lent money to their own customers to buy their equipment.

57
00:03:31,145 --> 00:03:32,915
Sales looked incredible.

58
00:03:33,225 --> 00:03:38,596
Then the customers ran out of cash, and the sales turned out to be borrowed from the future.

59
00:03:39,145 --> 00:03:41,556
To be fair, this is not the same.

60
00:03:41,866 --> 00:03:46,836
Most of Nvidia's sales still come from the richest companies on earth, paying cash.

61
00:03:47,066 --> 00:03:51,716
By one bank's estimate, the loop touches about thirteen percent of revenue.

62
00:03:51,786 --> 00:03:57,195
But that thirteen percent sits in the part of the AI industry that loses the most money.

63
00:03:57,225 --> 00:04:02,596
If it stumbles, Nvidia loses a customer and an investment on the same day.

64
00:04:04,502 --> 00:04:07,312
Which leads to the biggest question of all.

65
00:04:07,382 --> 00:04:10,711
Who is paying for this, and for how long?

66
00:04:10,901 --> 00:04:18,351
Two years ago, the biggest cloud companies spent about two hundred and sixty billion dollars on buildings and equipment.

67
00:04:18,221 --> 00:04:22,432
This year, Wall Street expects around eight hundred billion.

68
00:04:22,262 --> 00:04:25,312
Next year, more than a trillion.

69
00:04:25,182 --> 00:04:28,192
A very large part of that lands at Nvidia.

70
00:04:28,262 --> 00:04:31,391
And that spending is not a law of nature.

71
00:04:31,182 --> 00:04:36,351
It is a choice, made every three months, by about five CEOs.

72
00:04:36,141 --> 00:04:39,951
They will keep making it only if the profits show up.

73
00:04:39,781 --> 00:04:44,992
So far, by some estimates, the return on each new dollar is shrinking.

74
00:04:44,822 --> 00:04:47,872
And more of it is being paid for with debt.

75
00:04:47,781 --> 00:04:50,271
Here is why that is dangerous.

76
00:04:50,141 --> 00:04:52,672
Nvidia does not sell a subscription.

77
00:04:52,661 --> 00:04:54,351
It sells equipment.

78
00:04:54,422 --> 00:05:00,432
If a customer buys the same amount next year as this year, Nvidia's growth is zero.

79
00:05:00,262 --> 00:05:06,031
If they buy a little less, because what they already built is enough for now, Nvidia shrinks.

80
00:05:05,901 --> 00:05:08,992
Even if AI itself is doing fine.

81
00:05:08,822 --> 00:05:10,752
Railroads went through this.

82
00:05:10,541 --> 00:05:12,591
So did fiber-optic cable.

83
00:05:12,422 --> 00:05:14,432
The technology was real.

84
00:05:14,262 --> 00:05:16,112
It changed the world.

85
00:05:15,942 --> 00:05:22,192
And the companies selling the equipment crashed anyway, because the buyers stopped to catch their breath.

86
00:05:22,021 --> 00:05:25,391
Nvidia's stock price assumes nobody stops.

87
00:05:27,290 --> 00:05:32,019
The fourth crack is one Nvidia did not cause and cannot fix.

88
00:05:32,089 --> 00:05:38,180
A few years ago, Nvidia had about ninety-five percent of the AI chip market in China.

89
00:05:38,089 --> 00:05:44,019
Then Washington decided those chips were too powerful to sell to a rival, and blocked them.

90
00:05:43,850 --> 00:05:51,379
Jensen Huang summed up the result in one sentence: "We went from ninety-five percent market share to zero."

91
00:05:51,209 --> 00:05:53,699
Losing those sales hurts.

92
00:05:53,529 --> 00:05:56,259
What replaced them is worse.

93
00:05:56,089 --> 00:06:03,459
Chinese companies still needed chips, so they turned to Huawei, which analysts estimate now holds about half that market.

94
00:06:03,290 --> 00:06:09,379
A rival with a protected home market, guaranteed customers, and its government behind it.

95
00:06:09,290 --> 00:06:12,100
That is how a future competitor gets built.

96
00:06:14,002 --> 00:06:16,172
The last crack is the newest.

97
00:06:16,482 --> 00:06:18,771
Nvidia is Jensen Huang.

98
00:06:18,802 --> 00:06:22,971
He has run it since day one, for more than thirty years.

99
00:06:23,041 --> 00:06:27,132
When investors buy this stock, they are buying one man's judgment.

100
00:06:27,601 --> 00:06:35,411
In early November, according to reports from several major news outlets, Huang told employees he had been unfaithful to his wife.

101
00:06:35,442 --> 00:06:45,091
The reports say the relationship was with an executive at a company that buys Nvidia's chips, and that U.S. officials believe that person had ties to Chinese intelligence.

102
00:06:45,201 --> 00:06:48,012
Those are allegations, and nothing has been proven.

103
00:06:48,401 --> 00:06:53,451
What is confirmed is that Nvidia's board has opened an independent investigation.

104
00:06:53,521 --> 00:06:56,651
The stock dropped about ten percent in two days.

105
00:06:57,201 --> 00:07:02,172
Hundreds of billions of dollars, over a question about one person.

106
00:07:02,641 --> 00:07:05,932
That is what it means to be a one-man company.

107
00:07:07,846 --> 00:07:09,776
So is this the fall?

108
00:07:10,247 --> 00:07:16,137
History has one answer you need to hear, and it is not the one either side wants.

109
00:07:16,326 --> 00:07:20,817
In March of 2000, the most valuable company in the world was Cisco.

110
00:07:21,047 --> 00:07:26,576
It sold the essential equipment for the technology everyone knew was the future: the internet.

111
00:07:26,887 --> 00:07:29,216
And everyone was right about the internet.

112
00:07:29,526 --> 00:07:36,137
Cisco's stock fell more than eighty percent anyway, and it stayed below that peak for more than two decades.

113
00:07:36,166 --> 00:07:38,016
The company never went bankrupt.

114
00:07:38,086 --> 00:07:40,656
It is still big and profitable.

115
00:07:40,726 --> 00:07:44,377
But people who bought at the top waited a generation.

116
00:07:44,487 --> 00:07:47,536
That is what a fall looks like for a company like this.

117
00:07:47,526 --> 00:07:48,857
Not bankruptcy.

118
00:07:48,887 --> 00:07:52,257
A great business with a price that was too high.

119
00:07:52,406 --> 00:07:54,096
But here is the difference.

120
00:07:54,086 --> 00:07:59,137
At its peak, Cisco cost around two hundred times its yearly profit.

121
00:07:58,966 --> 00:08:03,697
Nvidia, after this drop, costs closer to twenty-five.

122
00:08:03,526 --> 00:08:06,257
Cisco's price was built on hope.

123
00:08:06,086 --> 00:08:10,016
Nvidia's is built on profits that exist right now.

124
00:08:09,846 --> 00:08:12,257
The question is whether they last.

125
00:08:14,255 --> 00:08:16,346
So here is the honest answer.

126
00:08:16,735 --> 00:08:19,146
One crack, Nvidia survives.

127
00:08:19,375 --> 00:08:20,906
It has survived worse.

128
00:08:21,136 --> 00:08:28,026
It nearly died in the nineties, and its stock was cut in half in 2008 and again in 2022.

129
00:08:27,855 --> 00:08:38,145
Two cracks at once, the spending pause and customers with a real alternative, and you get Cisco: a strong company and a lost decade for shareholders.

130
00:08:38,255 --> 00:08:40,905
A true fall needs all five.

131
00:08:40,975 --> 00:08:46,346
What is new is that, for the first time, all five are visible at the same moment.

132
00:08:46,576 --> 00:08:50,466
On November seventeenth, Nvidia reports its next results.

133
00:08:50,495 --> 00:08:52,745
That is the first real test.

134
00:08:52,816 --> 00:08:58,385
For thirty years, Jensen Huang has said his company is thirty days from going out of business.

135
00:08:58,416 --> 00:09:01,826
He said it to keep people scared when everything was going right.

136
00:09:01,936 --> 00:09:04,985
Nobody at Nvidia needs reminding this month.
